{"attack": true, "case": "The target asserts that surviving attempts to falsify a theory is what 'make[s] it the most informative error-eliminated option available.' This conflates a theory's corroboration (surviving tests) with its informative content. In critical rationalism, informative content (empirical content or falsifiability) is an a priori logical property determined by the set of basic statements the theory forbids, not a property acquired or maximized by surviving tests. A severely tested survivor is not more informative than a newly proposed, highly falsifiable conjecture; it is simply more corroborated. Because the target bases its preference for the survivor on a property (informativeness) that survival does not confer, its defense of the critical rationalist wager provides an incoherent Popperian justification.", "counterexample": null, "premise": "In critical rationalism, a theory's informative content is an a priori logical property determined by what the theory rules out, and surviving severe tests increases its corroboration but not its informative content.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "5ba7a0652869a36673791dc2f6fff77a7a20626eb0d4d0034c8ffe46ddd9754b", "quote": "we choose the best-tested theory not because it is likely true, but because it has survived attempts to falsify it, making it the most informative error-eliminated option available."}], "successor_question": "Does the survival of severe tests increase the logical informative content of a theory, or merely its degree of corroboration?"}